{"name":"Watermill Wood","hex":"#d3cccd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"watermill","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mill powered by water.\nThe machine or device itself.","The machine or device itself."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/watermill"},{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Watermill","blocks":[{"text":"A watermill or water mill is a mill that uses hydropower. It is a structure that uses a water wheel or water turbine to drive a mechanical process such as milling (grinding), rolling, or hammering. Such processes are needed in the production of many material goods, including flour, lumber, paper, textiles, and many metal products. These watermills may comprise gristmills, sawmills, paper mills, textile mills, hammermills, trip hammering mills, rolling mills, and wire drawing mills."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Braine-le-Ch%C3%A2teau_JPG02.jpg/330px-Braine-le-Ch%C3%A2teau_JPG02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watermill"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/WaterMill_Interior_MotionBlur.jpg/500px-WaterMill_Interior_MotionBlur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/WaterMill_Interior_MotionBlur.jpg/1280px-WaterMill_Interior_MotionBlur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/WaterMill_Interior_MotionBlur.jpg/500px-WaterMill_Interior_MotionBlur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Interior of the Lyme Regis watermill, UK (14th century)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWaterMill_Interior_MotionBlur.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Roda_de_Vitruvi.jpg/500px-Roda_de_Vitruvi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Roda_de_Vitruvi.jpg/500px-Roda_de_Vitruvi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Model of a Roman water-powered grain mill described by Vitruvius. The millstone (upper floor) is powered by an undershot waterwheel by the way of a gear mechanism (lower floor)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoda_de_Vitruvi.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Roman_mill_at_Chemtou.jpg/500px-Roman_mill_at_Chemtou.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Roman_mill_at_Chemtou.jpg/1280px-Roman_mill_at_Chemtou.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&